From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 07:54:31 -0400
Subject: mac signing: a Developer ID build can be signed and notarized in one
 run

Chromium's signer assessed the freshly signed app with Gatekeeper before
notarizing it. On current macOS every Developer ID app is rejected until
it is notarized ("rejected, source=Unnotarized Developer ID"), so the
assessment failed every run, and its error path raised NameError on a
missing import instead of reporting the command. The first Developer ID
run of this series stopped there (2026-09-23).

Two upstream faults and one change:
- signing.py imports subprocess, so a failed verification is reported.
- Distribution.to_config now carries the notarization level into the
  per-distribution config. It was dropped, so every distribution config
  read the default, STAPLE, whatever --notarize said; the pkg step's
  --timestamp followed the default too.
- validate_app assesses with Gatekeeper only when no notarization
  follows. After stapling, tooling/sign-release assesses and fails unless
  the answer is "accepted, source=Notarized Developer ID".

Tests: the two sign_chrome cases that expect an assessment now sign
without notarization, and a new case checks that signing before
notarization does not assess.

Why: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md "Signing"; BACKLOG S-17.
Removable when: upstream carries the level into the distribution config
and moves the assessment after stapling, or Stedding signs elsewhere.
